WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.280
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:08.950
You know that I am always excited to tell
you about news where AI players manage to

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:11.799
beat humans at more and more complex games.

00:00:11.799 --> 00:00:17.060
Today we are going to talk about DOTA 2 which
is a multiplayer online battle arena game

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:23.010
with a huge cult following and world championship
events with a prize pool of over 40 million

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.110
dollars.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:29.320
This is not just some game, and just to demonstrate
how competitive it is and how quickly it is

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:35.019
growing, last time we talked about this in
Two Minute Papers episode 180 where an AI

00:00:35.019 --> 00:00:39.929
beat some of the best players of the game
in a limited 1 versus 1 setting, and the prize

00:00:39.929 --> 00:00:42.800
pool was 20 million dollars back then.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.949
This was a huge milestone as this game requires
long-term strategic planning, has incomplete

00:00:47.949 --> 00:00:53.300
information and a high-dimensional continuous
action space, which is a classical nightmare

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.390
situation for any AI.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:01:00.789
Then, the next milestone was set to defeat
a human team in the full 5 versus 5 game,

00:01:00.789 --> 00:01:04.619
and I promised to report back when there is
something new on this project.

00:01:04.619 --> 00:01:05.909
So here we go.

00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:10.090
If you look through the forums and our YouTube
comments, it is generally believed that this

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:13.350
is so complex that it would never ever happen.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:19.250
I would agree that the search space is indeed
stupendously large and the problem is notoriously

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:23.880
difficult, but whoever thinks this will never
be solved has clearly not been watching enough

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:25.090
Two Minute Papers.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:30.180
Now, you better hold on to your papers right
away, because this video dropped 10 months

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:38.759
ago, in August 2017, and since then, the AI
has played 180 years worth of gameplay against

00:01:38.759 --> 00:01:41.230
itself every single day.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:42.230
&lt;sigh&gt;

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:47.820
80% of these games it played against itself,
and 20% against its past self, and even though

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:52.960
five of these bots are supposed to work together
as a team, there is no explicit communication

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.400
channel between them.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.210
And now, it is ready to play 5 versus 5 matches.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:03.409
Some limitations still apply, but since then,
the AI was able get a firm understanding of

00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:08.539
the importance of teamfighting, predicting
the outcome of future actions and encounters,

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:14.140
ganking, or in other words, ambushing unsuspecting
opponents and many other important pieces

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.140
of the game.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:20.650
The May 15th version of the AI was evenly
matched against OpenAI's in-house team, which

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:25.379
is a formidable result, and I find it really
amusing that these scientists were beaten

00:02:25.379 --> 00:02:26.940
by their own algorithm.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.890
This is, however, not a world class DOTA 2
team.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:35.040
And the crazy part is that the next version
of the AI was tested three weeks later, and

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:40.530
it not only beat the in-house team easily,
but also defeated several other teams and

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.620
a semi-professional team as well.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:47.799
As it is often incorrectly said on several
forums that these algorithms defeat humans

00:02:47.799 --> 00:02:55.099
because they can click faster, so I will note
that that these bots perform about 150-170

00:02:55.099 --> 00:02:59.640
actions per minute, which is approximately
in line with an intermediate human player,

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:04.530
and it is also to be noted that DOTA2 is not
that sensitive to this metric.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:08.390
More clicking does really not mean more winning
here at all.

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:12.739
The human players were also able to train
with an earlier version of this AI.

00:03:12.739 --> 00:03:17.980
There will be an upcoming event on July 28th
where these bots will challenge a team of

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:21.200
top players, so stay tuned for some more updates
on this!

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:26.190
There is no paper yet, but I've put a link
to a blog post and the full video in the description,

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:30.500
and it is a gold mine of information and was
such a joy to read through.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.100
So, what do you think?

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.100
Who will win?

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:38.420
And is a 5 versus 5 game in DOTA 2 more complex
than playing Starcraft 2?

